    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "One-word verb times",
        "body": "What do you think you will discover today about “Spanish verb times: one word or two?”?\n\nA **verb** names an action. To **conjugate** means to change it for who speaks and for **when** it happens. In the indicative mood we tell facts as real stories, not wishes. With **cantar** (to sing): **canto** is now, **cantaba** is a past that keeps going, **canté** is a closed past, **cantaré** is later, **cantaría** is «would.» The **stem** is cant-; the **ending** finishes the word. Those five forms are all **one word** each. Practice: Your turn. Write canto, cantaba, canté, cantaré, and cantaría with yo. Say what time each one points to.\n\nMira este ejemplo. Look at this example. Say one sentence about what you learned.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Práctica: Ahora te toca a ti.\n\nCommon mistake: Error común:"
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Two-word verb times",
        "body": "Sometimes Spanish uses **two words**: a form of **haber** (he, has, ha…) plus the **participle**, the already-done form ending in -ado or -ido. Here **haber** is a helper, not «there is.»\n\nMira este ejemplo. **He cantado** means the singing already happened and still matters now. **Había comido** means someone had already eaten before another past moment. The participle stays the same for he, she, or we—it does not gain -s for plural.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Your turn. Write he cantado and había comido. Underline the participle in each pair.\n\nCommon mistake: Say hemos comido, not hemos comidos."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "One word or two?",
        "body": "Choose by **when** and whether the result still matters. **Ayer terminé** uses one word because yesterday closed the action. **Ya he terminado** uses two because the result is still alive now.\n\nMira este ejemplo. **Leía** when **sonó** the phone mixes background (one word) with a sudden closed hit (another one word). Time words like ayer, ya, and cuando are clues—not rules you can ignore.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Your turn. Write one sentence with one verb word and one with two verb words about your week. Draw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verb times: one word or two”. Add one label from today's lesson.\n\nCommon mistake: With a closed «yesterday,» do not force «ayer he ido.»\n\n## Drawing for the image band\nDraw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verb times: one word or two”. Add one label from today's lesson."
      }
    ],
